WEBVTT

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.490
Here’s how to run the skills marketplace.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.790
It shows what the team can offer each other, and where people want to grow.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.770
Plan about thirty minutes.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.990
Give everyone a sheet: their own market stall.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.110
On it, they write three things they can offer the team, and one thing they want to grow in.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.170
Offers can be anything useful.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:30.250
Running a workshop, building a spreadsheet, calming down an angry customer, explaining things simply, knowing everyone in finance.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.730
Give people five quiet minutes to write, and ask them to be concrete.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.550
“Good at communication” is hard to use.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.710
“I can help you shorten a long email” is easy.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.370
Then go round the room.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.950
Each person reads their stall out loud, in a minute or two.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.610
Put the stalls on the wall next to each other, like a real market.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.710
Now look at the market together.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.870
Ask: “Where do you see a match?

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.650
Who could learn from whom?”

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.670
Draw lines between an offer on one stall and a wish on another.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:05.610
You’ll usually find more matches than you expect, and people are often surprised by what their colleagues can do.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:11.150
Then comes the most important step: agree one pairing to try in the next few weeks.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.370
Who learns what, from whom, and when they’ll meet.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.090
Write it on the canvas.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.470
Without that, the market stays a nice list on a wall.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.110
With it, someone actually learns something, and the team gets a little stronger.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.170
Ask about it in your next team meeting: “How did the pairing go?”

